---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const services = [
  { name: 'Vaultwarden', desc: 'Password vault', port: ':8081' },
  { name: 'Gitea', desc: 'Self-hosted git + CI', port: ':8087' },
  { name: 'Headscale', desc: 'Self-hosted mesh control plane', port: ':8086' },
  { name: 'Pi-hole', desc: 'DNS ad-blocking', port: ':8053' },
  { name: 'Samba + Syncthing', desc: 'Storage + sync', port: ':445/:22000' },
  { name: 'Uptime Kuma', desc: 'Service monitoring', port: ':8082' },
  { name: 'Netdata', desc: 'Real-time metrics', port: ':19999' },
];
---

<Base title="Homelab Case Study">
  <p class="mono-chip inline-block mb-5" style="font-size:0.95rem;padding:0.35rem 0.9rem">case-study/homelab &middot; 84 commits &middot; 1 raspberry pi</p>
  <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-6 text-text leading-tight">
    I built a datacenter on a <span class="grad-text">spare $50 board</span>.<br />Then I deleted most of it.
  </h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-text-muted max-w-2xl mb-16">
    This is the honest version &mdash; not the "look how many containers I run" flex.
    It's a story about wanting to own my own stuff, over-building to prove I could,
    realising I'd made it too complex, and learning that the senior move is subtraction.
  </p>

  <!-- Stat band -->
  <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-6 mb-20 reveal">
    <div class="border border-surface-light rounded-2xl px-6 py-4 bg-surface-light/20">
      <div class="font-display text-4xl font-bold grad-text">30&rarr;9</div>
      <div class="mono-chip inline-block mt-2" style="font-size:0.82rem">containers: peak vs. live</div>
    </div>
    <div class="border border-surface-light rounded-2xl px-6 py-4 bg-surface-light/20">
      <div class="font-display text-4xl font-bold grad-text">2&rarr;1</div>
      <div class="mono-chip inline-block mt-2" style="font-size:0.82rem">nodes: mesh vs. one pi</div>
    </div>
    <div class="border border-surface-light rounded-2xl px-6 py-4 bg-surface-light/20">
      <div class="font-display text-4xl font-bold grad-text">6&rarr;1</div>
      <div class="mono-chip inline-block mt-2" style="font-size:0.82rem">metrics stack &rarr; netdata</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- THE STORY -->
  <section class="mb-20 max-w-2xl">
    <div class="reveal mb-12">
      <p class="mono-chip inline-block mb-3" style="font-size:0.85rem">00 — the itch</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold mb-3">I didn't want to rent my own life.</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Google Drive gives you 15GB free, then it's pay-to-continue. iCloud is $5/month for 200GB,
        or around $15/month for 2TB &mdash; forever, for storage I'd already paid for once. My
        passwords, my files, my photos, my DNS all lived on someone else's server, rented indefinitely.
      </p>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        I did the maths. A Raspberry Pi 4B &mdash; even at today's ~$180 &mdash; plus an SSD pays for
        itself inside about three years versus those subscriptions, runs on a few dollars of electricity
        a year, and does <em>far</em> more than storage. And I didn't even need to buy one: I had a
        <span class="text-accent font-medium">spare 4GB Pi 4B from 2021 (about $50 at the time) sitting in a drawer</span>,
        so I paired it with a 2TB SATA SSD and a small fan cooler and started building.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal mb-12">
      <p class="mono-chip inline-block mb-3" style="font-size:0.85rem">01 — naive ambition</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Fresh graduate. I over-built everything.</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        As a new grad I figured: if I'm going to run a server, I should use it to its absolute fullest.
        So I went all in &mdash; nearly <strong>30 containers</strong>: authentication, monitoring,
        secrets management, dashboards, the works. On paper it looked like a real production platform,
        and honestly it felt great to have built it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Then reality caught up. A 4GB Pi was completely overburdened carrying all of that at once.
        <span class="text-accent font-medium">Lesson one: capability isn't the same as a good idea.</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal mb-12">
      <p class="mono-chip inline-block mb-3" style="font-size:0.85rem">02 — the offload</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold mb-3">So I threw hardware at it.</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        My first instinct wasn't to cut &mdash; it was to add more machine. I moved the heavy containers
        off the Pi onto my Windows desktop (32GB RAM, Ryzen 7 4800H) for the heavy lifting and mesh-linked
        the two. <strong>Eleven heavy containers</strong> migrated across; the Pi finally breathed again.
        It worked. It also quietly doubled the complexity.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal mb-12">
      <p class="mono-chip inline-block mb-3" style="font-size:0.85rem">03 — the honest look</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold mb-3">"Two-node mesh" sounded better than it was.</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        The Pi-plus-Windows mesh was impressive in name only. Too many moving parts, too much complexity,
        and I still wasn't using half of what I'd stood up. Worst of all, it chained my always-on Pi to a
        laptop I actually use every day &mdash; a 24/7 dependency on a machine that needs to sleep, move,
        and get shut down. <span class="text-accent font-medium">Lesson two: fewer moving parts beats more capability.</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal mb-4">
      <p class="mono-chip inline-block mb-3" style="font-size:0.85rem">04 — the maturity move</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold mb-3">I kept only what I actually use.</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        I migrated and retired everything that wasn't earning its place, and self-hosted the last piece I
        didn't control &mdash; the mesh coordinator itself &mdash; so nothing external could lock me out.
        What's left is nine services on one Pi: easy to manage, low overhead, and genuinely aligned with
        how I work day to day.
      </p>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        I didn't <code class="font-mono-accent text-accent">rm -rf</code> the rest &mdash; everything
        decommissioned moved through a holding folder, documented in a decision record, recoverable via git
        history. <span class="text-accent font-medium">Lesson three: the flex isn't the build, it's the
        restraint.</span> Juniors add. Seniors remove.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Receipts -->
  <section class="mb-20 reveal">
    <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold mb-2">The <span class="grad-text">receipts</span></h2>
    <p class="text-text-muted mb-6">Not a mockup. Live numbers from the box, right now.</p>
    <div class="terminal text-sm">
      <div class="terminal-bar">
        <span class="terminal-dot" style="background:#ff5f56"></span>
        <span class="terminal-dot" style="background:#ffbd2e"></span>
        <span class="terminal-dot" style="background:#27c93f"></span>
        <span class="ml-2 text-xs text-text-muted">vansh@autobot: ~ &mdash; status</span>
      </div>
      <div class="p-5 leading-relaxed">
        <p><span class="text-accent" style="font-size:1.15rem;font-weight:700">$</span> uptime-kuma --summary</p>
        <p class="text-text-muted mb-3">
          10 / 10 monitors <span style="color:#22c55e">UP</span> &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 98&ndash;100% uptime across the board
        </p>
        <p><span class="text-accent" style="font-size:1.15rem;font-weight:700">$</span> netdata --collected</p>
        <p class="text-text-muted mb-3">5,095 metrics live &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 1 node &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 14d high-res retention</p>
        <p><span class="text-accent" style="font-size:1.15rem;font-weight:700">$</span> uname -a &amp;&amp; nproc &amp;&amp; free -h</p>
        <p class="text-text-muted">
          Debian aarch64 (kernel 6.18) &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 4 cores @ 1.50GHz &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 3.71GB RAM &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 2TB SSD
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-3">
      Every service self-hosted on one Raspberry Pi 4B, monitored by itself, reachable only over an encrypted mesh.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Live services -->
  <section class="mb-20 reveal">
    <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold mb-2">What's <span class="grad-text">actually</span> running</h2>
    <p class="text-text-muted mb-6">Nine services. Each at its own address, over an encrypted mesh &mdash; no reverse proxy, no single point of failure.</p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      {services.map((svc) => (
        <div class="card-lift border border-surface-light rounded-2xl p-5 bg-surface-light/20">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-1">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-base">{svc.name}</h3>
            <span class="font-mono-accent text-sm text-accent font-medium">{svc.port}</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-muted">{svc.desc}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Closing -->
  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="border border-surface-light rounded-2xl p-8 bg-brand/5">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold mb-3">What this actually says about how I work</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-5">
        Operating under a hard constraint. Evaluating real trade-offs instead of reaching for the
        buzzword. Catching a security issue in my <em>own</em> work &mdash; a live API key leaked in
        client-side JavaScript &mdash; and fixing it the same day. And being willing to reverse a
        decision out loud instead of quietly walking it back. That's the engineer I am.
      </p>
      <a href="https://github.com/iamvanshkhanna/homelab-ops-mesh" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-full bg-cta text-white font-semibold hover:bg-cta-hover transition-colors">
        Read the real commit history &rarr;
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
